  "source": {
    "citation": "No adequate published general-population normative table for strict pull-ups. Shape informed by US military and law-enforcement fitness test distributions and by reported adult upper-body strength decline.",
    "url": "",
    "population": "None. This is a construction, not a normative sample.",
    "provisional": true,
    "transcription_verified": false,
    "notes": "PROVISIONAL, and the hardest test in the battery to norm honestly. Pull-ups are zero-inflated: a large share of adults, and a majority of women past 40, cannot do one. A mean and SD are meaningless against that shape, so this file uses cut-points, and places the 0-rep point at HALF the estimated zero share rather than at its top. That is deliberate. If 60 per cent of women aged 40-44 cannot do a pull-up, a zero cannot be resolved any finer than \"somewhere in that 60 per cent\", and the midpoint is the least wrong single answer - putting it at the top would hand every zero-scorer the 60th percentile. Consequence: a zero is scored generously relative to a true ranking, and the first rep moves the percentile a long way. Each value appears at exactly one percentile: publishing 0 reps at both the 30th and the 50th says nothing extra and leaves the edge slope undefined. Re-fit from app data first."
  },
